#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""LinuxMissions mock systemd toolkit (python3 stdlib only, Python >= 3.8).

One multi-call program. The name it is run under picks the tool:

    systemctl   journalctl   systemd-analyze   timedatectl

(`systemd-mock TOOL ARGS...` works too.) A level's setup.sh copies the tools it
needs into the sandbox with plain `cp` (which follows the symlinks in
mocks/systemd/). The tools treat a "fake root" as "/": /etc/systemd/system
means $ROOT/etc/systemd/system. $ROOT is $LM_ROOT, or else the directory that
holds the tool (a trailing bin/, sbin/, usr/ or local/ is skipped when that
directory has no etc/).

Mock-only data lives in $ROOT/.lm/: `now` (frozen clock), `boot-timing`
(systemd-analyze data), `boot_id`, and systemd/ (runtime state). The journal is
JSON lines (`journalctl -o json`) in $ROOT/var/log/journal/*.json plus the
entries the mock itself writes to $ROOT/run/log/journal/runtime.json.

Output formats and exit codes follow systemd 255 on Ubuntu 24.04. See
mocks/README.md for everything a level author needs.
"""
